CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is an isometric visualization of a single layer's mapping, drawn as two flat square planes stacked one above the other and linked by dashed trajectories. Both planes are outlined in heavy dark gray and carry faint pale gray cross lines through their centers as axis guides. On the lower plane a broad cloud of red dots is scattered over the surface, densest near the middle and thinning toward the corners. Red dashed curves rise from each dot, converging as they climb, and land on the upper plane, where the points no longer fill an area at all: they are packed edge to edge along one short horizontal segment near the plane's back edge, forming a nearly solid red line. Light gray dashed lines trace the frame connecting the corners of the two planes, and a pair of gray arrowheads extends outward along the upper plane in line with the collapsed points. Holding both planes in the same orientation and scale makes the change of shape immediate, as a two-dimensional spread on the input is squeezed onto a one-dimensional locus on the output.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
